[38;5;231m[49m⏺[39m [1mSection 2: Data flow and error handling[0m

  [1mFlow:[0m the user submits the form → [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m → [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m returns [38;5;153m{ success, user, userId }[39m → if
  [38;5;153msuccess[39m, [38;5;153msession.setCurrentUser({ userId, username: user })[39m → later, any form calls [38;5;153msession.getCurrentUser()[39m to find
  out who's logged in.

  [1mError handling:[0m
  - [1mFailed login[0m ([38;5;153msuccess: false[39m): the session isn't touched. Whoever was logged in before stays logged in. A failed
    attempt by someone else on the same browser shouldn't log out the current user.
  - [1mCorrupt stored data[0m (bad JSON, or [38;5;153muserId[39m missing because the user edited localStorage): [38;5;153mgetCurrentUser()[39m returns
    [38;5;153mnull[39m and deletes the bad entry. Forms treat that as "not logged in" and never crash on stored data.
  - [1mStorage not available[0m (Safari private mode, quota errors, storage disabled): [38;5;153msetCurrentUser[39m catches the error, logs
    it with [38;5;153mconsole.error[39m (the existing style in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m) and doesn't throw. The login still works for the current page;
    it just doesn't persist. [38;5;153mgetCurrentUser[39m returns [38;5;153mnull[39m.
  - [1mMissing [38;5;153muserId[39m when setting the user:[0m [38;5;153msetCurrentUser[39m throws a [38;5;153mTypeError[39m. That's a programming bug rather than a
    runtime condition, so failing loudly is right.

